Description
There is a resource leak in guava-testlib/.../testing/FakeTickerTest.java. In the runConcurrentTest method, an ExecutorService is created using newFixedThreadPool. However, there is no call to executorService.shutdown() or shutdownNow() at the end of the method. Since newFixedThreadPool creates non-daemon threads by default, these threads will remain alive in the JVM even after the test method finishes, potentially leading to thread exhaustion or OOM errors in large test suites.
Example
// Location: com.google.common.testing.FakeTickerTest.java

private void runConcurrentTest(int numberOfThreads, Callable<@Nullable Void> callable) throws Exception {
  ExecutorService executorService = newFixedThreadPool(numberOfThreads);
  // ... tasks submission ...
  doneLatch.await();

  // CRITICAL MISSING: executorService.shutdown();
  // Threads are leaked here.
}
Expected Behavior
The ExecutorService should be explicitly shut down, preferably inside a try-finally block, to ensure all threads are released after the test completes.
try {
    // ... test logic ...
} finally {
    executorService.shutdown();
}
Actual Behavior
The ExecutorService remains active, and its worker threads leak, persisting until the JVM terminates.
